\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Ginsparg–Wilson operators are pairs of gradings}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{For a \vocabk{graded-hermitian}{fdip-0148} #{D} on a
finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space with self-adjoint
grading #{\Gamma} and spacing #{a>0}, the Ginsparg–Wilson
relation holds exactly when #{\Gamma'=\Gamma(\bbI-aD)} is a
self-adjoint grading. Conversely every self-adjoint grading
#{\Gamma'} gives such an operator
#{D=a^{-1}(\bbI-\Gamma\Gamma')}. Then #{\bbI-aD} is unitary,
#{D} is normal, and #{\Gamma D=-D\Gamma'}.}

\p{Write #{\mathcal H_{st}} for the simultaneous eigenspace
with signs #{s} for #{\Gamma} and #{t} for #{\Gamma'}. Their
orthogonal complement #{\mathcal H_0} is stable under both
gradings, and each has trace zero there. The kernel of #{D}
is #{\mathcal H_{++}\oplus\mathcal H_{--}}, while
#{\ker(D-2a^{-1}\bbI)=\mathcal H_{+-}\oplus\mathcal H_{-+}}.
On #{\mathcal H_0} each eigenvalue #{\lambda} satisfies
#{|1-a\lambda|=1} and #{\lambda\ne0,2a^{-1}}.}

\proof{
\p{Graded hermiticity gives #{(\Gamma')^\dagger=\Gamma'}.
Expanding yields
##{(\Gamma')^2-\bbI=-a\Gamma(\Gamma D+D\Gamma-aD\Gamma D).}
Conversely #{D=a^{-1}(\bbI-\Gamma\Gamma')} satisfies
#{D^\dagger=\Gamma D\Gamma} whenever #{\Gamma'} is
self-adjoint. Thus the square identity gives the equivalence
in both directions. Rearranging the quadratic relation gives
#{\Gamma D=-D\Gamma'}. The product #{\Gamma\Gamma'=\bbI-aD} has
adjoint and inverse #{\Gamma'\Gamma}. Conjugation by #{\Gamma}
also gives that inverse. Since the product commutes with its
adjoint, #{D} commutes with #{D^\dagger}.}

\p{For a complex Hermitian operator, apply the real spectral
decomposition of \ref{fdip-002R} with inner product
#{\operatorname{Re}\langle x,y\rangle}. Its real eigenspaces
are stable under multiplication by #{\ui}. Orthogonality to
both #{y} and #{\ui y} in the real inner product gives complex
orthogonality, so complex orthonormal bases in the eigenspaces
form one for the whole space. The Hermitian operators
#{(\Gamma\Gamma'+\Gamma'\Gamma)/2} and
#{(\Gamma\Gamma'-\Gamma'\Gamma)/(2\ui)} commute.
The second preserves every eigenspace of the first. Diagonalizing
its Hermitian restriction on each gives a common orthonormal basis.
Adding the first operator to #{\ui} times the second shows that
this basis diagonalizes #{\Gamma\Gamma'}.}

\p{For a nonzero eigenvector #{\Gamma\Gamma'v=zv}, unitarity
gives #{|z|=1}. Then #{(\Gamma\Gamma')\Gamma v
=\Gamma(\Gamma\Gamma')^{-1}v=z^{-1}\Gamma v}, so #{\Gamma}
exchanges the eigenspaces of #{z} and #{z^{-1}}. Since
#{\Gamma'v=z\Gamma v}, the second grading exchanges
the same pair of spaces. For nonreal #{z} both gradings have
zero diagonal blocks on the pair, hence trace zero. For
#{z=\pm1} the eigenspace is #{\Gamma}-stable. Splitting it by
the grading projectors of \ref{fdip-003O} gives the joint
spaces, since #{\Gamma'=z\Gamma} there. Finally
#{D=a^{-1}(\bbI-\Gamma\Gamma')} acts by #{a^{-1}(1-z)} on each
such eigenspace. This gives the assertions on the orthogonal sum.}
}

\p{For a finite-dimensional complex normal operator, TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{LinearMap.exists_orthonormalBasis_apply_eq_smul_of_isStarNormal}
supplies an orthonormal eigenbasis. The exchange of the eigenspaces
under the two gradings gives the trace statement above.}
